2852 0 obj
<<
  /Title (Simulation-based cutoffs for conditional item fit in Rasch models: iterations, multiplicity correction, and decision stability)
  /Subject (Simulation-based cutoff values for conditional item fit can be estimated by parametric bootstrapping and reported as an interval of a chosen width. Johansson (2025) recommended this over rule-of-thumb critical values but gave iteration guidance without accounting for the error rate that the interval width controls. This paper re-examines iterations and width against the familywise error rate (FWER), and compares FWER and false discovery rate control, in seven simulation studies with dichotomous and polytomous items. The interval width is shown to set the FWER of the interval rule jointly with the number of items. Bootstrap p-values with a Westfall-Young step-down target that error rate directly and detect more misfit than a width-matched interval. When all items fit, their FWER was at or slightly below the nominal level from 400 iterations, and the small excess at fewer iterations came from how the statistics are studentized. Holm performed similarly once iterations cleared its floor, which grows with the number of items, while Benjamini-Hochberg flagged more fitting items. When some items misfit, every procedure exceeded its nominal rate at large samples, because misfit contaminates the total score that conditional fit is computed on. Conditional outfit was less powerful than infit throughout. Recommendations include flagging items on the Westfall-Young corrected p-value, using at least 400 iterations and 1000 to 2000 for a final analysis, reporting the interval as a descriptive band at width .95, and removing items one at a time. The methods are available in the R package easyRasch2 and the jamovi module easyRasch2jmv.)
  /Keywords (Rasch, Psychometrics, Item fit, Cutoffs, Critical values, Multiple comparisons, Reproducibility)
  /Author (Magnus Johansson)
  /Creator (Typst 0.14.2)
  /ModDate (D:20261103095039+01'00)
  /CreationDate (D:20261103095039+01'00)
>>
endobj

